  "history": "Prospect Park, located in Brooklyn, New York City, was designed by landscape architects Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux, who also co-designed Central Park. Construction began in 1866 and was completed in 1873. The park was officially opened to the public on October 31, 1867, though it was still under development for several more years. Inspired by the English landscape garden style, the designers aimed to create a naturalistic, scenic space that provided urban residents with a peaceful retreat from the city's density. Key features include the 90-acre (360,000 m²) park's rolling terrain, the 1.3-mile (2.1 km) Long Meadow, the 320-foot (98 m) Grand Army Plaza arch, and the 60-foot (18 m) tall Shelburne Arch. The park's development was part of the broader 19th-century movement to create public green spaces in rapidly growing American cities. Over time, Prospect Park became a vital community hub, hosting cultural events, recreational activities, and serving as a symbol of Brooklyn's identity. It was designated a National Historic Landmark in 1966 and remains one of NYC's most iconic parks today."
